nError at line 53. Good.\n\nAlso \"'hello\" (unterminated): q = \"'\", s[:3] = \"'he\" != \"'''\" \u2192 q = \"'\". s.endswith(\"'\")? \"hello\" ends with 'o' \u2192 False \u2192 AssertionError at line 52. Good.\n\nAnd \"hello\" (no quotes): line 48 assertion fails. Good.\n\nFor hex errors: evalString(r\"'\\x4'\") raises ValueError \"invalid hex string escape\". And evalString(r\"'\\xzz'\") raises ValueError. These cover escape's error branches \u2014 bonus coverage.\n\nFor octal: all regex-matched octal tails are valid 0-7 digits so int(tail, 8) always succeeds; the ValueError branch is unreachable via evalString. Skip.\n\nNow, about state pollution: evalString is a pure function; no monkeypatching needed. But the instructions emphasize cleanup. Since there's no state, I'll just note nothing to clean \u2014 but I shouldn't add explanation outside the code block. I can add a brief comment in the code. Actually the response must be ONLY the python code block. I can include comments inside the code.\n\nImport: `from blib2to3.pgen2.literals import evalString`. \n\nLet me double-check the escape regex behavior for `r\"'a\\tb'\"`:\n- s = \"'a\\\\tb'\" (raw string in test source: r\"'a\\tb'\" is the 6-char string 'a\\tb' with quotes)\n- q = \"'\", not triple\n- endswith \"'\" \u2192 yes\n- s = \"a\\\\tb\" \u2192 re.sub replaces \\t with tab \u2192 \"a\\tb\" (actual tab). Expected: \"a\\tb\" written as \"a\\tb\" in test (actual tab). In test code I'd write `evalString(r\"'a\\tb'\") == \"a\\tb\"` \u2014 the right side \"a\\tb\" contains a real tab. Good.\n\nFor `r\"'\\x41'\"`: s = \"'\\\\x41'\", inner = \"\\\\x41\", regex matches \"\\\\x41\" with tail \"x41\" \u2192 int('41', 16) = 65 \u2192 'A'. Good.\n\nFor `r\"'\\x411'\"`: inner = \"\\\\x411\", regex `x.{0,2}` greedy matches \"x41\", tail \"x41\" \u2192 'A', remaining \"1\" \u2192 \"A1\". Good.\n\nFor `r\"'\\101'\"`: tail \"101\" \u2192 int('101', 8) = 65 \u2192 'A'. Good.\n\nFor `r\"'\\1'\"`: tail \"1\" \u2192 1 \u2192 chr(1) = '\\x01'. Good.\n\nFor `r\"'\\777'\"`: 511 \u2192 chr(511) = '\u01ff'. Maybe skip that to avoid unicode confusion; use `\\101` and `\\1`.\n\nAlso mixed: `r\"'\\x41\\n\\102'\"` \u2192 'A' + '\\n' + 'B'. int('102', 8) = 66 = 'B'. Good.\n\nNow the assertion-failure tests:\n- `pytest.raises(AssertionError, evalString, \"hello\")` \u2014 line 48 assert fails.\n- `pytest.raises(AssertionError, evalString, \"'hello\")` \u2014 line 52 assert fails.\n- `pytest.raises(AssertionError, evalString, \"'''\")` \u2014 line 53 assert fails.\n\nHmm wait, for \"'''\": Actually let me re-check. s = \"'''\" (3 apostrophes). s.startswith(\"'\") \u2192 True. q = \"'\". s[:3] == \"'''\" \u2192 True \u2192 q = \"'''\". s.endswith(\"'''\") \u2192 True. len(s) = 3 >= 2*3 = 6 \u2192 False \u2192 AssertionError. Correct.\n\nWhat about `\"'a'\"` where content has an escaped quote at the end: `r\"'\\'\"` \u2014 s = \"'\\\\''\" (4 chars: quote, backslash, quote, quote). q = \"'\". s[:3] = \"'\\\\'\" != \"'''\" \u2192 q = \"'\". endswith \"'\" \u2192 True. len 4 >= 2 \u2192 True. inner = \"\\\\'\" \u2192 regex matches \\\\' \u2192 tail \"'\" \u2192 simple_escapes[\"'\"] = \"'\" \u2192 result \"'\". So evalString(r\"'\\'\") == \"'\". That's a nice edge case. Include it.\n\nAlso double-quote inside single quotes without escape: evalString(\"'\\\"'\") == '\"'. Fine.\n\nNow, one more consideration: the module path. The code is from blib2to3/pgen2/literals.py, so `from blib2to3.pgen2.literals import evalString` should work.\n\nRegarding cleanup/state pollution: evalString is pure \u2014 no monkeypatch needed. I'll add a comment noting that. The instructions say to make sure to clean up; since the function is pure, there's nothing to clean up, and I can state that in a comment.\n\nLet me also make sure each test has assertions verifying postconditions \u2014 yes, all the equality tests use assert. The raises tests use pytest.raises which asserts the exception is raised; I can also check the message with `match=`.\n\nFor the ValueError tests:\n- evalString(r\"'\\x4'\") \u2192 ValueError with message \"invalid hex string escape ('\\\\x4')\". In regex match string: r\"invalid hex string escape \\('\\\\x4'\\)\". Let me be careful with escaping in match. The message is: `invalid hex string escape ('\\x4')`. Using `match=r\"invalid hex string escape \\('\\\\x4'\\)\"` \u2014 match uses re.search on the string representation of the exception. The actual message contains a backslash before x4. In a raw string r\"\\\\x4\" represents backslash-backslash-x-4? No wait: r\"\\\\x4\" is the two characters `\\\\` then `x4`? In a raw string, r\"\\\\x4\" is literally backslash backslash x 4 \u2014 4 characters. As a regex, `\\\\` matches a single literal backslash, then `x4` matches x4. So the regex r\"\\\\x4\" matches the text `\\x4`. Good.\n\nActually simpler: use `match=\"invalid hex string escape\"` without the specific escape. That avoids regex escaping headaches. But being specific is better for correctness verification. Let me use:\n\n```python\nwith pytest.raises(ValueError, match=r\"invalid hex string escape \\('\\\\x4'\\)\"):\n```\n\nHmm, the message: `\"invalid hex string escape ('\\\\%s')\" % tail` \u2192 for tail = \"x4\", message = `invalid hex string escape ('\\x4')`. The regex to match: `invalid hex string escape \\('\\\\x4'\\)`. In raw string: r\"invalid hex string escape \\('\\\\x4'\\)\". Let's parse: `\\\\` matches literal backslash, then `x4` matches, `\\(` matches `(`, `\\)` matches `)`. Wait order: the message is `('\\x4')` \u2014 chars: ( ' \\ x 4 ' ). The regex r\"\\('\\\\x4'\\)\" parses as: `\\(` matches `(`, `'` matches `'`, `\\\\` matches `\\`, `x4` matches `x4`, `'` matches `'`, `\\)` matches `)`. \n\nFor the invalid hex with letters: evalString(r\"'\\xzz'\") \u2192 tail \"xzz\", len(hexes)=2, int('zz', 16) raises ValueError \u2192 caught \u2192 raises ValueError \"invalid hex string escape ('\\xzz')\". Good.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 does the regex match