\\t\\v\". Good.\n\n`test_quote_and_backslash_escapes`: r\"'\\'x\\\"y\\\\z'\" \u2014 the raw string is: ' \\ ' x \\ \" y \\ \\ z '. Wait, in raw string r\"'\\'x\\\"y\\\\z'\", the characters are: ' \\ ' x \\ \" y \\ \\ z '. Hmm, but the string starts with ' and the second char is \\, third is '. So s = ['\\', '\\\\', \"'\", 'x', '\\\\', '\"', 'y', '\\\\', '\\\\', 'z', \"'\"]. \n\nWait, that's a problem: s[0] = \"'\", s[:3] = \"'\\\\'\" (quote, backslash, quote) \u2014 is that equal to \"'''\"? No. So q = \"'\". s.endswith(\"'\") \u2192 last char is ' \u2192 True. len(s) = 11 >= 2 \u2192 True. inner = s[1:-1] = \"\\\\'x\\\\\\\"y\\\\\\\\z\" i.e. backslash-quote-x-backslash-quote-y-backslash-backslash-z.\n\nRegex substitution: \n- `\\'` \u2192 `'` (simple_escapes[\"'\"] = \"'\")\n- `x` literal\n- `\\\"` \u2192 `\"` \n- `y` literal\n- `\\\\` \u2192 `\\`\n- `z` literal\n\nResult: `'x\"y\\z`. Expected in test: \"'x\\\"y\\\\z\" which is the string `'x\"y\\z`. Good.\n\nHmm wait, but there's a subtlety: the regex alternation `\\\\(\\'|\\\"|\\\\|...)` \u2014 for the input `\\\\'x...`: at position 0, backslash followed by `'` \u2192 matches `\\'` alternative first (alternation is ordered). Yes \u2192 `'`. Good.\n\n`test_trailing_escaped_quote_in_single_quoted_string`: r\"'\\'\" \u2014 characters: ' \\ ' '. s[0] = \"'\", s[:3] = \"'\\\\'\" \u2260 \"'''\" \u2192 q = \"'\". endswith \"'\" \u2192 True. len 4 >= 2 \u2192 True. inner = s[1:-1] = \"\\\\'\" (backslash, quote). Regex: `\\\\'` matches \u2192 `'`. Result: \"'\". Expected: \"'\" \u2014 the string containing one apostrophe. In test: `evalString(r\"'\\'\") == \"'\"`. Good.\n\nWait, careful: r\"'\\'\" as a Python raw string literal \u2014 r\"'\\'\" is valid: quote, backslash, quote, quote? No! r\"'\\'\" \u2014 the raw string starts with ', then \\, then ' \u2014 but wait, in a raw string, `\\'` doesn't escape the quote for termination purposes... Actually in Python, raw strings still treat backslash as escaping the quote for t