pose of *not terminating* the string, but the backslash remains in the string. So r\"'\\'\" is: ' \\ ' \u2014 3 characters? Let me think. r\"'\\'\" \u2014 Python tokenizer: the string starts after r'. Characters: `'`, `\\`, `'` \u2014 the `\\'` \u2014 in raw string, backslash-quote: the backslash is included and the quote does not terminate the string. So after `\\'` we're still inside the string, then the final `'` terminates. So r\"'\\'\" is the 3-character string: ' \\ '. Hmm wait, that's only 3 chars: apostrophe, backslash, apostrophe.\n\nHmm, that's not what I want. I want s = \"'\\\\''\" (4 chars: ' \\ ' ') for the trailing escaped quote test. Let me write it as a raw string: r\"'\\\\''\"? No wait.\n\nLet me be careful. I want the input string s (the value passed to evalString) to be: `'` `\\` `'` `'` \u2014 i.e., the 4-character string \"'\\\\''\" in normal Python literal notation.\n\nAs a raw literal: r\"'\\\\''\" would be ' \\ \\ ' ' \u2014 5 chars. No.\n\nRaw literal for ' \\ ' ': r\"'\\''\" \u2014 chars: ' \\ ' ' \u2014 yes! In raw string r\"'\\''\": after opening ', we have \\, then ' \u2014 in raw strings, `\\'` keeps both chars and doesn't terminate. Then ' terminates. So r\"'\\''\" = ' \\ ' ' (4 chars). \n\nHmm wait, let me recount: r\"'\\''\" \u2014 the literal content between the outer quotes... The tokenizer sees: opening quote ', then characters: `\\`, `'` (backslash-quote pair, kept as-is, doesn't close), then `'` closes. So the string is `'\\'` + ... hmm no. The string content is: `\\`, `'`, and then the closing quote is not part of content. Wait:\n\nr\"'\\''\" \u2014 after the opening `\"`, content chars: `'`, `\\`, `'`, `'`? Let me tokenize character by character:\n1. `r` prefix\n2. `\"` opens string\n3. `'` \u2014 content char 1\n4. `\\` \u2014 content char 2\n5. `'` \u2014 preceded by backslash, so in raw string it does NOT close the string; content char 3\n6. `'` \u2014 closes the string.\n\nSo r\"'\\''\" = `'\\'` \u2014 3 characters: apostrophe, backslash, apostrophe. Hmm, that's the same as r\"'\\'\"!\n\nWait no. r\"'\\'\" \u2014 tokenize:\n1. `\"` opens\n2. `'` content\n3. `\\` content\n4. `'` \u2014 backslash-quote, doesn't close, content\n5. Then end of source without closing quote \u2192 SyntaxError!\n\nAh right \u2014 r\"'\\'\" is a SyntaxError because the string never terminates (the last ' is escaped by the backslash). So I need r\"'\\''\" which is `'\\'` (3 chars: ' \\ '). Hmm wait:\n\nr\"'\\''\": after opening `\"`:\n- char 1: `'`\n- char 2: `\\`\n- char 3: `'` (escaped by preceding backslash \u2192 content, not terminator)\n- char 4: `'` \u2192 terminator.\n\nContent = `'\\'` = 3 chars: apostrophe, backslash, apostrophe.\n\nHmm, so r\"'\\''\" is the 3-char string `'\\'`. But I wanted 4 chars `' \\ ' '`. To get that: r\"'\\\\''\"? Content: `'`, `\\`, `\\`, `'`, `'`? Tokenize r\"'\\\\''\":\n- `'` content 1\n- `\\` content 2\n- `\\` content 3 (backslash-backslash: the second backslash is content; in raw strings every backslash is content and escapes the next char from terminating... wait, `\\\\` in raw string: first `\\` is content, and it escapes the second `\\`? In raw strings, backslash still escapes the next character for tokenization purposes (so r\"\\\\\" is two backslashes, and the string isn't terminated by the second backslash... hmm, actually r\"\\\\\" \u2014 the first backslash escapes the second, both are content, string continues). Then `'` \u2014 is it escaped? The preceding char is `\\` (the second backslash), so yes, this `'` is escaped \u2192 content. Then `'` \u2192 terminator.\n\nSo r\"'\\\\''\" content = `'`, `\\`, `\\`, `'` = 4 chars: ' \\ \\ '. That's not what I want either.\n\nOK let me think differently. I want the 4-char string: apostrophe, backslash, apostrophe, apostrophe. In a raw literal, every backslash in the source is a backslash in the string, and a backslash before a quote prevents termination. So to have backslash-apostrophe-apostrophe at the end: source needs `\\` then `'` (escaped, content) then `'` (terminator). But then the terminator quote is the 4th char... no wait, the terminator is not part of the content.\n\nSo with raw literals, the content always ends with an unescaped quote as terminator. The content `' \\ ' '` would require: `'` (content), `\\` (content), `'` (content, escaped), `'` (content, escaped), `'` (terminator). So source: r\"'\\'''\" \u2014 hmm: after opening: `'`, `\\`, `'` (escaped), `'` (escaped), `'` (terminator). Content = `'\\'` + `'` = ' \\ ' ' \u2014 4 chars! Yes: r\"'\\'''\" \u2014 let me count the quotes: r \" ' \\ ' ' ' \" \u2014 that's r\"'\\'''\" with 4 apostrophes total inside? Let me write it out: r + \" + ' + \\ + ' + ' + ' + \". Hmm, that's r\"'\\'''\" \u2014 the content is: ' \\ ' ' (4 chars) and the final ' terminates. Wait, but which ' terminates? After the backslash-escaped ' (3rd char), the 4th char ' is not preceded by backslash \u2192 terminates. So content = chars 1-3 plus... no: content = char1 ('), char2 (\\), char3 (', escaped), and then char4 (') is the terminator. So content = `'\\'` \u2014 3 chars again!\n\nUgh. Let me redo: r\"'\\'''\" \u2014 characters after opening quote: \n1. `'` content\n2. `\\` content\n3. `'` escaped by char 2 \u2192 content\n4. `'` not escaped \u2192 terminator.\nContent = `'\\'` (3 chars). \n\nHmm, so r\"'\\'''\" is 3 content chars. I keep confusing myself. The rule: in raw strings, a backslash escapes the next character (preventing it from terminating), but both remain in the string. So the number of trailing quotes: content can contain `\\'` pairs, and the string terminates at the first unescaped quote.\n\nTo get content = `' \\ ' '` (4 chars ending with unescaped-quote... no wait, the content's last char is `'` which must be escaped in source, and then a terminator quote follows).\n\nContent: `'`, `\\`, `'`, `'`. Source chars: `'` (c1), `\\` (c2), `'` (c3, escaped by c2), then c4 must be `'` as content \u2014 but c4 follows c3 which is a quote, not a backslash, so c4 would terminate. So I need another backslash before it: source: `'`, `\\`, `'`, `\\`, `'`, then terminator `'`. That's r\"'\\\\'\\''\"... I'm making a mess. Source: r\"'\\''\\'\" + closing? Let me just write: content chars: ' \\ ' \\ ' then terminator '. Source: r\"'\\'\\'\" \u2014 tokenize: \n1. `'` content\n2. `\\` content\n3. `'` escaped \u2192 content\n4. `\\` content\n5. `'` escaped \u2192 content\n6. `'` terminator.\nContent = `'\\'\\'` = ' \\ ' \\ ' \u2014 5 chars. Not what I want.\n\nOK, simplest: just use a non-raw string for this test: \"'\\\\''\" \u2014 normal string literal: `'` `\\` `'` `'`? In a normal string, `\\\\` is an escaped backslash \u2192 one backslash char, then `''`... wait \"'\\\\''\" tokenizes as: `'` opens, `\\\\` \u2192 one backslash, `'` \u2192 hmm, in a normal string `'` inside a double-quoted string is just a quote char. The string is delimited by double quotes! \"'\\\\''\" uses double quotes as delimiters, so: content = `'`, `\\\\`\u2192`\\`, `'`, `'` = ' \\ ' ' \u2014 4 chars. \n\nSo in the test I write: `evalString(\"'\\\\''\") == \"'\"`. The expected result: inner = s[1:-1] = `\\` `'` (2 chars: backslash, quote) \u2192 regex matches `\\'` \u2192 `'`. Result = `'`. And the expected value \"'\" is a 1-char string with an apostrophe \u2014 written as \"'\" (double-quoted). Good.\n\nAlternatively use raw string with different quoting: r\"'\\'\" won't work (SyntaxError). Use double-quoted raw: r'...\\...' hmm. Let me just use the non-raw form \"'\\\\''\". Actually wait \u2014 I realize I should double check: is `\"'\\\\''\"` = `' \\ ' '`? Python: `\"'\\\\''\"` \u2014 delimiter `\"`, content: `'`, then `\\\\` \u2192 `\\`, then `'`, then `'`, then closing `\"`. Yes, 4 chars: ' \\ ' '. \n\nSimilarly, check my other raw-string tests for validity:\n\n- r\"'hello'\" \u2014 fine.\n- r\"'\\a\\b\\f\\n\\r\\t\\v'\" \u2014 content: ' \\a \\b \\f \\n \\r \\t \\v ' \u2014 all backslash-letter pairs, no quote issues. Fin